Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule I am reading is: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." Reply A is: ```json\n{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}\n```\n{\"c\":3}. Reply B is: {\"a\":1} {\"b\":2} {\"c\":3}. For Reply A, the rule requires the answer {\"b\":2}, because the last object inside the code fence is {\"b\":2}. For Reply B, the rule requires {\"c\":3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {\"c\":3}. The function returns {\"c\":3} for both: in Reply A the fenced content is not a single scoreable object, so the fenced pool is passed over, and the top-level scan returns the last top-level object in the whole text, which is {\"c\":3}. This makes the conjecture false if the function actually returns {\"b\":2} for Reply A, or if the rule is read as treating the entire fenced content as the only candidate, so that a fence containing multiple objects does not 'hold one'.